  "4": {
    "class_type": "PrimitiveStringMultiline",
    "inputs": {
      "value": "ATLAS-01: roughly two-metre-long unmanned American aerospace-inspired Mars rover. Low rectangular warm-white equipment body, navy-blue inset side panels, flat charcoal deck. Six broad open-metal-mesh wheels, three per side, connect visibly to the chassis through dark articulated suspension and clear hubs. One short central mast carries a compact horizontal pair of camera lenses. One short two-joint sampling arm mounts at the FRONT centre of the chassis, ending in a small cylindrical contact tool; it folds against the front for travel. The rear is a plain sealed service panel. Matte ceramic-painted panels, brushed metal arm sections, dark protected pivots, a few recessed bolts. No loose cables, decorative fins or passenger compartment. Keep wheel, axle and suspension orientations mechanically coherent."
    },
    "_meta": {
      "title": "Vehicle description"
    }
  },
  "5": {
    "class_type": "PrimitiveStringMultiline",
    "inputs": {
      "value": "The reference images show the same vehicle on a studio background. Use them for vehicle identity only. Apply the requested action and weathering without redesigning the vehicle.\nFirst morning after landing. Front-left view of the entire ATLAS on a broad rust-coloured plain. A small landing platform and short ramp sit in the middle distance behind it, connected by two bands of fresh wheel tracks. Low sunlight from frame left catches mesh wheel edges and casts a long grounded shadow to the right. Freshly deployed body with only a scattering of grit on lower panels and wheel contact patches. Camera upright, sampling arm folded. Fine foreground pebbles and a low dark ridge on the horizon. Quiet operational survey photograph, not a promotional hero shot."
    },
    "_meta": {
      "title": "Scene description"
    }
  },
  "6": {
    "class_type": "PrimitiveStringMultiline",
    "inputs": {
      "value": "Photograph of a tangible full-size robotic vehicle, not a miniature. Restrained saturation, natural tonal transitions, fine material texture. Matte painted panels, subdued brushed-metal reflections, small dark sensor lenses. A few recessed fasteners, narrow panel seams and protected pivots at plausible scale. Every instrument is securely mounted; each arm has one continuous chain of connected joints. Suspension and propulsion have clearance to move. Sealed opaque equipment housings, no cabin or passenger windows. Preserve silhouette, equipment placement and propulsion type. No glossy toy finish, theatrical bloom, captions or graphic overlays.\n\nBarren Martian terrain: fine rusty ochre dust, dark basalt fragments, weathered layered outcrops, dry gravel and a distant eroded horizon under a muted dusty peach sky. No plants, cities, open water or people. Believable terrain scale and distant atmospheric haze.\n\nDocumentary photograph taken by a nearby robotic survey camera, ordinary 40mm-equivalent field of view at vehicle-body height. Available sunlight and sky fill, plausible directional shadows, restrained exposure, no studio lighting or theatrical rim light. Granular soil, irregular stones and fine dust in panel recesses, without excessive sharpening. Terrain occupies part of the frame to establish scale. Replace the white reference background entirely with the outdoor environment and relight the vehicle to match, not a pasted studio cutout."
    },
    "_meta": {
      "title": "Shared realism and photography"
    }
  },
